--- !u!114 &208436904
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 208436902}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 30e8e37d88289204784a0442b3c88612, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  steps:
  - Connect (220 VAC) terminal and the neutral path (N) of all used modules to the
    (220 VAC) terminal and the neutral path (N) of the main board.
  - Connect an Ethernet cable from the PV Solar Panel 3 (Comm. Out) to the PV Solar
    Panel 2 module (Comm. In).
  - Connect an Ethernet cable from the PV Solar Panel 2 (Comm. Out) to the PV Solar
    Panel 1 module (Comm. In).
  - Connect an Ethernet cable from the PV Solar Panel 1 (Comm. Out) to the Main Control
    Board module (Comm. In).
  - 'Connect three solar panels in parallel then connect Bypass Diodes in series
    with them as follows:

    1.Connect the (+VE) terminal of panel 1 to (+VE) terminal
    of solar panel 2 to (+VE) terminal of solar panel 3.

'
  - 2.Connect the (-VE) terminal of panel 1 to (-VE) terminal of solar panel 2 to
    (-VE) terminal of solar panel 3.
  - 3.Connect (+VE) terminal of solar panel 3 to (A) terminal of the diode
  - Connect the (C) terminal of the diode to the (1) terminal of the Rheostat 100-ohm
    module.
  - And connect the (-VE) terminal of the panel 1 module to the (3) terminal of the
    Rheostat 100-ohm module
  - Release the emergency stop button on the main board.
  - Raise the circuit breaker (C.B) of the main board.
  - Switch on the main board ON/OFF switch.
  - Release the emergency button of the PV Solar Panel.
  - Switch on the PV Solar Panel ON/OFF
  - Switch the Run/Stop selector to Run state.
  - Switch to the control software on the connected PC
  - Navigate to the "Measurements Graph" tab
  - Select the graph of the DC Current vs. DC voltage.
  - Gradually decrease the rheostat's resistance (increase the load) by turning its
    knob counter clockwise. Observe that the current increases while the voltage
    drops.
  - Switch to the software monitor again. The I-V characteristic curve will be drawn
    on the graph corresponding to the recorded points.
  - You have finished! You can now select another experiment.
  popups:
  - afterStep: 6
    text: A bypass diode is used in parallel with each solar panel to prevent power
      loss and overheating in case of partial shading or panel failure. The bypass
      diode provides an alternate path for the current, allowing the other panels
      to continue operating efficiently and protecting the system from voltage drops
      and potential damage.
  - afterStep: 8
    text: Always ensure emergency buttons are released before powering on any module.
  - afterStep: 17
    text: You can choose "Enable" button and use the slider to adjust the light dimming
      power of the solar panel from the software and observe the voltage and current
      values.
  - afterStep: 19
    text: In parallel connection, total current equals the sum of individual panel
      currents, while voltage remains unchanged.
  - afterStep: 19
    text: "Note that you can: \r\n\tExport all recorded measurements to an Excel
      sheet by clicking the \"Export Data\" icon.\r\n\tSave the screen by pressing
      the \"Save Screen\" icon.\r\n\tClear the graph using the \"Clear\" icon\r"
  stepItemPrefab: {fileID: 6570533322573217691, guid: cd25d304eaf4809488e7c6b299d13404, type: 3}
  contentParent: {fileID: 208436903}
